<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Postmenopausal bleeding for three weeks in a 60-year-old journalist, about eight years after her last period and with no hormone use. An office endometrial biopsy was taken today and a transvaginal ultrasound is booked this week. Please see her within two weeks to review both results with her and plan treatment if they show cancer or atypical hyperplasia. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks; sooner if the biopsy shows cancer. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>If the biopsy shows cancer, what are the histology, grade, molecular class and stage, and what further staging is needed?</item><item>What surgery do you recommend, including how the lymph nodes should be assessed?</item><item>Which treatment after surgery would the final stage and molecular class call for?</item><item>How should Lynch syndrome be screened for, and does she or her blood relatives need genetic testing?</item><item>What monitoring should primary care share during and after treatment?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Ifill, 60, is seen at the request of Dr. Achebe-Price two weeks after she first reported three weeks of postmenopausal bleeding. An office endometrial biopsy was taken at that visit, and a transvaginal ultrasound two days later. The biopsy shows endometrioid adenocarcinoma, and CT was done the following week. The bleeding is now occasional spotting; she has no pain, bowel or bladder change, or leg swelling. She takes no hormones. She works long days as a journalist and anchor and wants to keep working through treatment if possible, and to decide who is told. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Gwendolyn Ifill was born in Queens, New York, on September 29, 1955, the fifth of six children. Her father, a Panamanian immigrant, became a pastor in the African Methodist Episcopal Church; her mother was from Barbados. The family moved around the Northeast for her father&apos;s work and for a time lived in federally subsidized housing. [D]</item><item>She graduated from Simmons College in Boston in 1977. As a college intern at the Boston Herald American she found a racist note left for her; the paper&apos;s editors hired her after she graduated. She went on to report for the Baltimore Evening Sun, The Washington Post, The New York Times and NBC News. [D]</item><item>In 1999 she became moderator of PBS&apos;s Washington Week in Review, the first African American woman to host a major national political talk show, and a senior correspondent for the NewsHour. In 2013 she and Judy Woodruff were named co-anchors of the PBS NewsHour, the first women to jointly lead a national nightly news broadcast. [D]</item><item>She moderated the vice-presidential debates of 2004 and 2008. In the 2004 debate she asked the candidates about AIDS in the United States, citing the much higher death rate among Black women aged 25 to 44. She wrote The Breakthrough: Politics and Race in the Age of Obama (2009). [D]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

