<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Endometrioid adenocarcinoma of the endometrium, FIGO grade 2, mismatch repair deficient (C54.1). The biopsy shows a low-grade endometrioid cancer that has lost MLH1 and PMS2, with a wild-type p53 pattern. The enlarged right pelvic and left para-aortic nodes on CT suggest stage IIIC disease; the final FIGO 2023 stage comes from surgery. The molecular class needs POLE sequencing on the surgical specimen as well: a POLE-mutated tumor carries an excellent outlook, a p53-abnormal one a poor outlook, and a mismatch repair deficient tumor responds strongly to immunotherapy. She is fit, and the disease on CT can be removed with a minimally invasive operation: total hysterectomy with removal of both tubes and ovaries, washings, sentinel lymph node mapping with indocyanine green, and removal of the enlarged pelvic and para-aortic nodes. If nodal disease is confirmed, I recommend dostarlimab with carboplatin and paclitaxel every three weeks for six cycles, then dostarlimab alone every six weeks for up to three years: for mismatch repair deficient advanced endometrial cancer this combination sharply lowers the risk of progression compared with chemotherapy alone. Radiation would be reserved for nodal disease left after treatment. The loss of MLH1 is the universal Lynch syndrome tumor screen; in most women of her age it comes from MLH1 promoter methylation in the tumor, which is acquired, not inherited. If methylation is found, germline testing is not needed; if it is not, she should have germline testing after counseling, and if a variant is found, her blood relatives should be offered cascade testing through a letter she can share. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Achebe-Price&apos;s intake note. Endometrial biopsy: endometrioid adenocarcinoma, FIGO grade 2; mismatch repair immunohistochemistry with loss of MLH1 and PMS2 and retained MSH2 and MSH6; p53 wild-type pattern. Transvaginal ultrasound: endometrium 16 mm, heterogeneous, with an irregular junction with the myometrium and internal flow; two small intramural fibroids; normal ovaries. CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis: enlarged uterine cavity; a 1.5 cm right external iliac node and a 1.7 cm left para-aortic node below the renal veins; no liver, lung, bone or peritoneal lesion; no ascites. Today: CA-125 28 U/mL; hemoglobin 12.1 g/dL, platelets 274; creatinine 0.80 mg/dL; albumin 4.1 g/dL. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Robotic-assisted total laparoscopic hysterectomy with removal of both tubes and ovaries, washings, sentinel lymph node mapping, and removal of the enlarged pelvic and para-aortic nodes, in two weeks. [S]</item><item>MLH1 promoter methylation testing on the biopsy now; POLE sequencing and estrogen receptor on the surgical specimen; FIGO 2023 stage with the molecular class recorded. [S]</item><item>Genetic counseling once the methylation result is back; germline testing only if MLH1 loss is not explained by methylation; cascade testing of blood relatives only if a germline variant is found. [S]</item><item>If nodal disease is confirmed: dostarlimab 500 mg with carboplatin AUC 5 and paclitaxel 175 mg/m2 every 21 days for six cycles, then dostarlimab 1000 mg every six weeks for up to three years, after hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV screening. [S]</item><item>Before each cycle: blood count, kidney and liver function, magnesium, glucose, TSH and free T4, and a morning cortisol; neuropathy and immune-related symptom checks at each visit; three-drug antiemetic prevention. [S]</item><item>CA-125 is normal and should not be used to follow her; CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis at the end of combination treatment. [S]</item><item>Survivorship care after treatment: examination with a pelvic examination every three months for two years and CT every six months for three years for stage III disease, with no routine vaginal cytology; lymphedema checks; distress screening at each visit. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Achebe-Price, who saw her the same day. The biopsy, ultrasound and CT were explained to Ms. Ifill, who chose surgery in two weeks; she decides who else receives this note. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Surgery in two weeks; review with the final pathology and the tumor board recommendation before the first cycle; Dr. Achebe-Price continues primary care and shares the monitoring. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
